[Kick La Meta | Senior Dev] This error suggests that the issue lies in how your video component is set up. It appears that you are trying to use the `useVideoConfig()` hook within a component that hasn't been properly registered as a `<Composition />` in your Remotion project.

To resolve this, make sure you have wrapped your video component with the `<Composition />` element. Here's an example of how your code might look:

```tsx
// video.tsx
import { Composition, useVideoConfig } from 'remotion';

const MyVideo: React.FC = () => {
  const { durationInFrames, width, height, fps } = useVideoConfig();

  return (
    <Composition durationInFrames={durationInFrames} width={width} height={height} fps={fps}>
      {/* Your video content here */}
    </Composition>
  );
};

export default MyVideo;
```

By wrapping your component with `<Composition />`, you provide the necessary context for the `useVideoConfig()` hook to work correctly. Make sure to adjust the `durationInFrames`, `width`, `height`, and `fps` props according to your video requirements.

Let me know if this resolves the issue or if there's anything else I can assist with!

[Denis | Developer] Thanks, that was it. But I just realized that I forgot to provide some more context. I'm working on a project called "ViolasSin" and it's a video editing tool for violin players. The tool helps them synchronize their performances with pre-recorded accompaniments. So, I'm dealing with videos and audio a lot.

Now, I'm trying to implement a feature that allows users to trim their video and audio files. I want them to be able to select a range within the video, and then I'll cut everything outside that range. I think I need to manipulate the durationInFrames prop somehow, but I'm not sure how to approach this. Could you guide me through the implementation? I'd really appreciate a detailed explanation or even a code snippet if possible!

